From 146abc7ac442d46fb5fed3d7e39ab648a65743aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 23:00:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] DisplayInfo: take the 12-byte version 1 Dolby block of
 display management 2 and 3

The rule took a 12-byte version 1 Dolby VSVDB with the low-latency field
1 only with display management 4 or later: the metadata sent was that of
content mapping v4.0, and nothing said whether an older display reads it.
Since the previous commit such a display is sent the content mapping
v2.9 part, which it reads, so the condition goes. What these displays are
sent does not otherwise change: with the low-latency field 1 the block
asks for the Dolby vendor InfoFrame, the one Hades sends to every display
it takes (Amlogic, CoreELEC linux-amlogic hdmi_tx_main.c,
hdmitx_set_vsif_pkt(): "ver1_12 with low_latency = 1 and ver2 use Dolby
VSIF").

In 584 public EDIDs with a Dolby block (the linuxhw/EDID dataset) 119
have such a block of display management 2 or 3, and got HDR10 until now:
LG TVs of 2017 and 2018 (43), TCL (25), Toshiba (20), Vestel (9), Vizio
(7, six of display management 2), Denon and Onkyo receivers (8), Hisense
(2), Roku (2) and three more.

What stays out is what asks for the older signalling, the HDMI 1.4b
vendor InfoFrame (24 bytes, OUI 00-0C-03), which no backend sends:
version 0 (Vizio, Sharp of 2015 to 2017), the 15-byte version 1 block
(the LG TVs of 2016) and the 12-byte one with the low-latency field 0.
The texts now say that: the reason in the log, the line of the display
information, and the Dolby Vision page ("older Dolby Vision signalling
(HDMI 1.4b), not sent"; en_gb, es_es, es_ar and es_mx).

The kernel backends read the block with the same rule (linux-9978); with
a kernel that does not, i915 and amdgpu refuse the tunnel for these
displays, say why, and Kodi keeps the ordinary output.

Not seen on a display of display management 2 or 3: none is in reach.
Tested on the host: Kodi's rule and the two kernel copies against what
an independent reading of the block forms says, on the 584 EDIDs, the
ten of the users' reports and the three preset blocks of Amlogic's
driver (597, 0 failures; the rule before this change fails on the 119),
and the three-way test of before (4513890 checks, 0 failures).
